{"name":"Shallow Shoal","hex":"#9dd6d4","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"shallow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having little depth; significantly less deep than wide.","Extending not far downward."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shallow portion of an otherwise deep body of water.","A fish, the rudd."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shallow"},{"term":"shoal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Shallow."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sandbank or sandbar creating a shallow.","A shallow in a body of water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shoal"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shoal","blocks":[{"text":"A shoal is a natural submerged ridge, bank, or bar that consists of, or is covered by, sand or other unconsolidated material, and rises from the bed of a body of water close to the surface or above it, which poses a danger to navigation. Shoals are also known in oceanography, geomorphology, and geoscience, as sandbanks, sandbars, gravelbars, or bars. Two or more shoals that are either separated by shared troughs or interconnected by past or present sedimentary and hydrographic processes are referred to as a shoal complex."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/GughSandbar.JPG/330px-GughSandbar.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shoal","wikidata":"Q28337"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/WayaWayasewa.jpg/500px-WayaWayasewa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/WayaWayasewa.jpg/500px-WayaWayasewa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A tidal sandbar connecting the islands of Waya and Wayasewa of the Yasawa Islands, Fiji","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWayaWayasewa.jpg"},